Faithful mathematical transcription from the matching one-page LaTeX source of the hashed PDF, 12 October 2026. Equations and all scope sections are included; layout commands are omitted.

Area measures transverse size. Acceleration length is the light-travel length of the surface-gravity timescale, $L=ct_\kappa$ with $t_\kappa=c/\kappa$. One boundary potential gives surface stress, force, and pressure.



Geometry and the mechanical responses

For a specified clock and positive surface gravity $\kappa$, use the horizon area $A$ and
\begin{equation}
 L=\frac{c^2}{\kappa},\qquad
 U(A,L)=\frac{A\kappa c^2}{8\pi G}
       =\frac{c^4}{8\pi G}\frac AL.
\end{equation}
The Einstein boundary structure fixes this potential [1]. With $c,G$ fixed,
\begin{equation}
 dU=\frac{c^4}{8\pi GL}\,dA-\frac{Ac^4}{8\pi GL^2}\,dL
    =\Pi\,dA-F_L\,dL.
\end{equation}
For the mass equivalent $M_U=U/c^2$, the responses and force density are
\begin{align}
 \Pi&=\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial A}\right)_L=\frac UA,
 & F_L&=-\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial L}\right)_A=\frac UL=M_U\kappa,\\[0.35em]
 P_L&=\frac{F_L}{A}=\frac{\Pi}{L}=\frac{\kappa^2}{8\pi G},
 & U&=\Pi A=F_L L=P_L AL.
\end{align}
The two terms change surface area and energy per area: $A\,d\Pi=-F_L\,dL$, or $d\Pi=-P_L\,dL$. Volume work uses the pressure paired with its displacement; $AL$ is not generally that volume.



Thermal translation and stationary horizons

The Hawking--Unruh temperature and Einstein area entropy give
\begin{equation}
 T=\frac{\hbar c}{2\pi k_B L},\qquad
 S=\frac{k_B A}{4\ell_P^2},\quad \ell_P^2=\frac{\hbar G}{c^3},\qquad
 U=TS,\quad \Pi\,dA=T\,dS.
\end{equation}
\textit{Rindler:} $L=c^2/a_{\rm obs}$ is the normal horizon distance on the instantaneous rest slice; planar patch area $A$ is independent. \textit{Schwarzschild:} $A=4\pi R^2$, $L=2R$, $U=Mc^2/2$, with time normalized at infinity. For areal volume $V=4\pi R^3/3$,
\begin{equation}
 F_L\,dL=F_R\,dR=M\kappa\,dR=P_R\,dV,\qquad
 P_R=2P_L=\frac{\kappa^2}{4\pi G}.
\end{equation}
Since $dL/L=dR/R$, this describes the same fractional horizon-size change. $R$ is areal radius, not a universal proper distance. Thus $dU=\Pi\,dA-P_R\,dV$, with $U=3P_RV$; area and volume co-vary. This reveals the mass-energy decomposition $Mc^2=TS+F_L L$, while retaining the standard mass first law $d(Mc^2)=T\,dS$.



Cosmological payoff

For the equilibrium FRW apparent horizon, $L=R_A$, so $P\equiv P_R=P_L$. With $V=4\pi R_A^3/3$, the Einstein--Misner--Sharp identification gives [2]
\begin{equation}
 U=E=\frac{c^4R_A}{2G}=\rho V,\qquad
 P=\frac{\rho}{3},\qquad
 \boxed{dE=T\,dS-P\,dV.}
\end{equation}
For flat FRW, $R_A=c/H$ and the expansion, acceleration, and conservation laws are
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{H^2=\frac{8\pi G}{c^2}P},\qquad
 \boxed{\frac{2\ddot a}{ac^2}=-\kappa_E(P+p_m)},\qquad
 \boxed{\dot P+H(3P+p_m)=0},
\end{equation}
Here $H=\dot a/a$, $\kappa_E=8\pi G/c^4$, $\rho$ is total bulk energy density and $p_m$ total bulk pressure, distinct from $P$; vacuum contributions are included consistently. For curved FRW, $R_A=c/\sqrt{H^2+kc^2/a^2}$ and $1/R_A^2=\kappa_EP$. These reorganize the Einstein--FRW equations.



Canonical basis and scope

With fixed clock, cuts, and the stated mixed boundary prescription, the scalar nonexpanding-null response ($\theta=\delta\theta=0$) is [1]
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\Theta^{(0)}_\kappa}{\Delta\tau}
 =\frac{Ac^2}{8\pi G}\,\delta\kappa
 =-\frac{Ac^4}{8\pi GL^2}\,\delta L=-F_L\,\delta L.
\end{equation}
This selected response per prescribed time is realized by stationary Rindler, Schwarzschild and de Sitter. It is not the full action variation. Evolving FRW retains its separate equilibrium-screen calibration; no complete moving-boundary canonical theorem is asserted. Thermal input is unnecessary for $F_L$ and $P_L$.


[1] Hopfm\"uller--Freidel, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.03096}{1611.03096}; Odak et al., \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.03854}{2309.03854}; Parattu et al., \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.1535}{1303.1535}.
[2] Cai--Kim, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0501055}{hep-th/0501055}; Akbar--Cai, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0609128}{hep-th/0609128}. Details and scope: the companion seven-page and full manuscripts.
